(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-cms-exam-2-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 62-year-old man is found on screening to have an intraocular pressure of 26 mm Hg in both eyes with an enlarged optic cup. He has no symptoms. Which visual field is lost first?","choices":["The superior field only","The inferior field only","The central field","The peripheral field","The temporal field of one eye only"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Central acuity is preserved until late, which is why it is found on screening rather than by the patient. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the prognosis of herpes simplex keratitis?","choices":["Good, though recurrences are common","It never recurs after the first episode","Uniformly poor with permanent scarring","It always progresses to corneal perforation","It resolves without any treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 59"},{"q":"A relative afferent pupillary defect is detected. What does its presence indicate about the cause of vision loss?","choices":["The refractive error is uncorrected","The eye is dry","The lens is opaque","The cornea is scarred","The optic nerve or extensive retina"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — it separates neural causes from media opacities. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has a swollen, red right upper and lower lid after a scratch three days ago. The globe is WHITE, eye movements are full and painless, vision is 6/6 and the pupils are equal. Temperature 37.8°C. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic lid oedema","Hordeolum","Dacryocystitis","Pre-septal cellulitis","Post-septal (orbital) cellulitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A white eye with full painless movements and normal vision is the single most useful discrimination in this block. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which structures does slit lamp examination assess?","choices":["The lids, cornea, conjunctiva, sclera and iris","The extraocular muscles","The visual pathway posterior to the chiasm","The optic nerve head only","The retina and choroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the anterior segment. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man develops floaters, flashes and a curtain over his vision two weeks after blunt ocular trauma. Which type of detachment is most likely?","choices":["Combined traction and exudative","Rhegmatogenous","Exudative","Serous from hypertension","Traction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a retinal break allows fluid beneath the retina, and trauma is a common cause. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with papilledema has a lumbar puncture. What finding is expected?","choices":["A raised white cell count","A raised opening pressure with normal constituents","A low opening pressure","A markedly raised protein with normal pressure","Xanthochromia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the pattern in idiopathic intracranial hypertension. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has reduced vision in the left eye. On the swinging flashlight test, both pupils constrict when the light is shone in the right eye, but both DILATE when the light is swung to the left eye. What does this indicate?","choices":["An Adie tonic pupil on the left","Horner syndrome on the left","An efferent defect on the left","A left relative afferent pupillary defect","Physiological anisocoria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The left optic nerve conducts less well, so swinging to it is a net reduction in light input. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 2-year-old girl is diagnosed with bilateral retinoblastoma. What does bilateral disease imply?","choices":["That it was caused by an infection","That no genetic counselling is needed","A heritable germline mutation","A better prognosis than unilateral disease","That the tumour is benign"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bilateral disease is germline, with implications for siblings and for second malignancies. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 42"},{"q":"How is retinoblastoma managed?","choices":["Topical chemotherapy alone","Spectacle correction","Observation with annual photographs","Laser trabeculoplasty","Multimodal therapy under ocular oncology"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — enucleation for large tumours. With genetic counselling. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which patients are named as at risk of bacterial conjunctivitis?","choices":["Only patients with allergies","Only those with recent surgery","The immunocompromised, elderly and children","Only newborns","Only contact lens wearers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Contact lens wearers as well. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man is found to have a relative afferent pupillary defect. Which of the following would explain it?","choices":["Horner syndrome","Optic neuritis","Third nerve palsy","Refractive error","A dense cataract"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a defect of the afferent pathway, which is the optic nerve. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy has an eye that turns inward. The cover test shows the left eye moving out to take up fixation when the right is covered. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure, as children outgrow this","Arrange surgery immediately","Refer to ophthalmology","Prescribe reading glasses and review in a year","Patch the deviating eye"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Manifest misalignment in a child needs assessment to prevent amblyopia. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 29"},{"q":"In roughly what fraction of FULL-THICKNESS lid lacerations is there an accompanying corneal laceration or globe rupture?","choices":["About two thirds","About one in ten","Almost none","Almost all","About one in twenty"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — which is why the globe is examined in every case. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which muscle does the fourth cranial nerve supply, and what is its action?","choices":["Levator palpebrae, which elevates the lid","Medial rectus, which adducts the eye","Lateral rectus, which abducts the eye","Inferior oblique, which elevates the adducted eye","Superior oblique, which depresses the adducted eye"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — hence vertical diplopia worse on downgaze. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman is diagnosed with adult inclusion conjunctivitis. Besides treating her eye, what else must be addressed?","choices":["Contact lens hygiene alone","Systemic treatment and partner notification","Allergen avoidance","Nothing further; topical treatment suffices","Lid hygiene twice daily"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the eye is a manifestation of a sexually transmitted infection. The genital infection has to be treated too. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a small, flat, uniformly pigmented choroidal lesion with drusen on its surface and no subretinal fluid. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Immediate treatment as a melanoma","Photographic surveillance","Urgent enucleation","Intravitreal anti-VEGF","Systemic chemotherapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Flatness, drusen and absent fluid all favour a benign nevus. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a choroidal melanoma asks where it would spread if it did. Which organ is the classic site?","choices":["The brain","The lung","The liver","Regional lymph nodes","Bone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — uveal melanoma spreads haematogenously, and the liver is the classic site. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 52-year-old patient has a burning sensation in both eyes. There has been no trauma and no contact with anyone with similar symptoms. Examination shows red, inflamed lid margins with greasy, matted lashes and dandruff-like debris at their bases. The conjunctivae are clear and there is no corneal involvement. Colonisation with which organism is found in a significant fraction of patients with this condition?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Moraxella catarrhalis","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Chlamydia trachomatis","Haemophilus influenzae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blepharitis is associated with S. aureus, and with rosacea and seborrhoeic dermatitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 44-year-old fisherman has a fleshy triangular growth extending from the nasal conjunctiva ONTO the cornea of his right eye. Vision in that eye has dropped slightly. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Conjunctival intraepithelial neoplasia","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Chemosis","Pterygium","Pinguecula"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The growth crosses the limbus onto the cornea, which is the whole distinction from a pinguecula. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has a swollen red eyelid, proptosis, pain on eye movement and diplopia. Temperature 39.0°C. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Topical antibiotic drops","Warm compresses and observation","Discharge on oral antibiotics with review in 48 hours","Admit for intravenous antibiotics and imaging","Refer to the allergy clinic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All post-septal disease is admitted, and imaging defines the extent. Computed tomography of the orbits and sinuses. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man on long-term corticosteroids has glare and trouble reading, and notes his vision improves once his pupils are dilated at the clinic. Which cataract type is this?","choices":["Cortical","Polar","Posterior subcapsular","Nuclear","Zonular"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — characteristically improves with dilation, more rapid in onset, and classically under fifty. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 36"},{"q":"A newborn develops a purulent conjunctivitis on day 2 of life. Which organism is most likely, given the timing?","choices":["Chlamydia trachomatis","Adenovirus","Neisseria gonorrhoeae","Staphylococcus aureus","Herpes simplex virus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gonococcal disease presents in the first few days; chlamydial disease presents later, around days 5 to 14. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman has sudden painless loss of vision in the left eye. She has had scalp tenderness when brushing her hair and aching in the jaw when chewing for three weeks. The erythrocyte sedimentation rate is markedly raised. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start high-dose corticosteroids immediately","Arrange outpatient review in two weeks","Start aspirin alone","Refer for carotid imaging first","Arrange temporal artery biopsy and treat once the result is back"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment precedes biopsy — the other eye is at risk within days. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 15-year-old girl cannot read the board at school but reads comfortably at her desk. Which refractive error is this, and how is the eye shaped?","choices":["Astigmatism, from an irregular cornea","Amblyopia, from visual deprivation","Presbyopia, from a stiffening lens","Hyperopia, with the eye too short","Myopia, with the eye too long"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Light focuses in front of the retina, so distance is blurred and near is clear. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman is diagnosed with open-angle glaucoma. Why is she unlikely to have noticed anything?","choices":["Central vision fails first and she has adapted","The disease causes no field loss at all","Loss is gradual and the other eye compensates","It only affects colour vision early","The pressure is normal in all cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why it is found on screening rather than by symptoms. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has painless, oval yellowish plaques on both upper lids near the inner canthus. Vision is normal. What is the most appropriate initial workup?","choices":["Thyroid function tests alone","Biopsy of the lesion","Fasting lipids, glucose and HbA1c","Slit lamp examination alone","Computed tomography of the orbits"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The local lesion is a marker; the underlying metabolic issue is what is treated. Liver function tests are checked alongside them. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has severe, boring right eye pain that wakes her at night, with a violaceous hue to the sclera. Phenylephrine does not blanch the vessels. Which form carries the greatest risk of perforation?","choices":["Diffuse anterior scleritis","Necrotising scleritis","Episcleritis","Nodular anterior scleritis","Posterior scleritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Perforation risk is greatest in the necrotising form rather than uniformly across scleritis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has bilateral disc swelling. What distinguishes papilledema from optic neuritis on examination?","choices":["Acuity is preserved in papilledema and reduced in optic neuritis","Papilledema causes a relative afferent pupillary defect","Papilledema is painful on eye movement","Papilledema causes colour desaturation early","Papilledema is always unilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and papilledema is bilateral while optic neuritis is usually unilateral with an afferent pupillary defect. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with idiopathic intracranial hypertension is started on treatment. Which measures are used?","choices":["Topical antiglaucoma drops","High-dose corticosteroids indefinitely","Antibiotics","Intravitreal anti-VEGF","Weight reduction and acetazolamide"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, with serial visual field monitoring. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which causes of hyperopia are named besides a naturally short eye?","choices":["Chronic sun exposure","Prolonged near work","Diabetes alone","Backward lens displacement, or a mass","Corticosteroid use"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — anything shortening the effective axial length. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man asks for a bottle of the numbing drops that relieved his corneal abrasion pain in the emergency department. Why should he not be given them?","choices":["They cause permanent pupil dilation","Repeated use is toxic and delays healing","They cause an allergic reaction in most patients","They raise intraocular pressure","They stain the cornea permanently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and it masks worsening infection. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has a suspected retinal detachment but the view of the fundus is poor. Which investigation is more sensitive than fundoscopy?","choices":["Fluorescein angiography","Gonioscopy","Ocular ultrasound","Magnetic resonance imaging","Tonometry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and it types the detachment as well. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 59-year-old myopic man reports a shower of new floaters and flashes of light in the right eye yesterday, followed today by a dark curtain rising across the lower half of his vision. There is no pain. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vitreous haemorrhage","Central retinal vein occlusion","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Retinal detachment"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Floaters and flashes followed by a painless curtain across the field, in a myopic patient. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has a sectoral area of redness in the right eye with mild discomfort but no pain and no visual change. Phenylephrine drops blanch the injected vessels. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis","Scleritis","Anterior uveitis","Episcleritis","Subconjunctival haemorrhage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Superficial, mildly uncomfortable, and the vessels blanch with phenylephrine. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with dacryocystitis has improved on oral antibiotics. What is usually needed afterwards?","choices":["Nothing further once the infection clears","Lifelong topical antibiotics","Annual imaging","Probing and irrigation of the system","Immediate excision of the lacrimal sac"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the underlying nasolacrimal duct obstruction remains, and surgery may follow. To check drainage. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 3-week-old infant is noted to have rhythmic to-and-fro eye movements present since birth. What is the significance of nystagmus at this age?","choices":["It confirms congenital cataract","It always indicates a brain tumour","It is a normal finding in newborns and needs no action","It resolves without evaluation by six months","It warrants evaluation for a cause of poor vision"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Congenital nystagmus often reflects an underlying visual deficit. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has a nail protruding from his left eye after a nail gun discharged. What should be done with the nail?","choices":["Remove it and irrigate the tract","Leave it in place","Remove it only once the patient is stable","Remove it under topical anaesthetic","Cut it flush with the cornea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It may be tamponading the wound, and removal risks extruding intraocular contents. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl has a white-eyed blowout fracture with entrapment. Why is urgent surgical referral required rather than routine review?","choices":["Entrapped muscle becomes ischaemic and fibroses","Vision will be lost immediately","The fracture will heal in the wrong position within hours","The eye will become infected","It always progresses to globe rupture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — delay risks permanent restriction of eye movement. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man was hit in the eye by a paintball. Examination shows blood layered in the lower part of the anterior chamber occupying about a third of it. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Irrigate the anterior chamber in the emergency department","Lie the patient flat and observe","Upright positioning, rigid shield and urgent ophthalmology review","Discharge with topical antibiotics","Start aspirin for the bruising"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — keeping the head up lets the blood settle inferiorly and clear the visual axis. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has painful swelling of the outer upper lid and the cause is not yet clear. Why must corticosteroids not be started immediately?","choices":["Infection must be reasonably excluded first","Steroids raise intraocular pressure within hours","Steroids would mask a lacrimal sac tumour","Steroids interact with topical antibiotics","Steroids are contraindicated in all lid disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — empiric oral antibiotics for 24 hours with reassessment is the alternative path. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which risk factors for myopia are named?","choices":["Family history and near work","Steroid use","Sun and wind exposure","Contact lens wear","Smoking and hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and trauma displacing the lens forward can cause it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has bilateral disc swelling. Which pressure is raised?","choices":["Both equally","Neither","Orbital venous pressure alone","Intracranial pressure","Intraocular pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the raised pressure is intracranial and NOT intraocular. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy of African ancestry has a traumatic hyphema. Which screening test is particularly important in his case?","choices":["Blood glucose","Thyroid function","Sickle cell testing","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","Serum calcium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sickling in the anterior chamber obstructs outflow and raises pressure at lower blood volumes. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which symptoms are named in idiopathic intracranial hypertension?","choices":["Jaw claudication and scalp tenderness","Headache, obscurations and intracranial noises","Severe eye pain with nausea and halos","Itching and stringy discharge","Flashes, floaters and a curtain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with pain behind the eyes and mild pain on eye movement. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man reports gradually blurring distance vision and increasing glare from oncoming headlights at night. He mentions he no longer needs his reading glasses. What explains the improvement in near vision?","choices":["Development of hyperopia","Onset of presbyopia","Improvement in accommodation with age","Reduced corneal astigmatism","A myopic shift from nuclear sclerosis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — 'second sight'. The hardening lens increases its refractive power. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which bones are named as sites of basilar skull fracture?","choices":["Maxilla and mandible","Cribriform plate, frontal, temporal, sphenoid","Nasal bones and vomer","Zygoma alone","Parietal bones only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Orbital plate of frontal, petrous or squamous temporal, and occipital. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 44"},{"q":"What holds blood in the anterior tissues in an orbital contusion?","choices":["The orbital floor","The tarsal plate and septum act as a wall","The lacrimal gland","The levator muscle","The conjunctiva"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — producing preseptal ecchymosis or haematoma. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has an orbital floor fracture with no entrapment and no ocular injury. What is the management?","choices":["Immediate surgical repair","Patching for six weeks","Admission for intravenous antibiotics","Ice, analgesia and review in a few days","Urgent canthotomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two to three days. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which local treatments for xanthelasma are named?","choices":["Lid hygiene","Warm compresses and massage","Intravitreal injection","Topical antibiotics","Cryotherapy, laser, peel or excision"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has proptosis, a tense orbit, severely reduced vision and a relative afferent pupillary defect one hour after facial trauma. What is the most appropriate immediate action?","choices":["Ice and observation","Immediate enucleation","Topical antiglaucoma drops alone","Urgent computed tomography before any intervention","Lateral canthotomy and cantholysis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — an orbital compartment syndrome needs decompression within minutes to save the nerve. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 33"},{"q":"Through which four stages does trachoma progress?","choices":["Flashes, floaters, curtain, then macular involvement","Papillae, chemosis, hypopyon, then perforation","Ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, then dilation lag","Drusen, pigment change, atrophy, then neovascularisation","Follicles, scarring, entropion, opacity"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which tissue is inflamed in episcleritis?","choices":["The sclera itself","The iris","The deep subconjunctival tissue","The lid margin","The cornea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the episcleral layer. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy treated for pre-septal cellulitis returns after 48 hours with new pain on eye movement and mild proptosis. What has happened?","choices":["He has developed an allergic reaction to the antibiotic","The infection has progressed behind the orbital septum","The diagnosis was always a chalazion","He has developed conjunctivitis","The swelling is simply resolving slowly"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — painful restricted movement and proptosis mean post-septal disease. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has a lid laceration 4 mm from the medial canthus after a dog bite. Why does that location matter?","choices":["It cannot be repaired surgically","The canalicular drainage may be cut","It means the levator muscle is involved","It always indicates a globe rupture","It indicates a basilar skull fracture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a missed canalicular injury leaves the patient tearing for life. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 64-year-old African American man presents after going blind in his right eye 'out of the blue' 20 minutes ago. There is no pain and he is not nauseated. He has had type 2 diabetes mellitus for 10 years. The left pupil reacts normally and the pressure is 17 mm Hg. The right pupil shows no reaction to light or accommodation, with a pressure of 20 mm Hg. Right ophthalmoscopy reveals arteriolar narrowing, vascular stasis and a 'boxcar' pattern. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Retinal detachment","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Macular degeneration","Central retinal artery occlusion","Subconjunctival haemorrhage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sudden, painless, monocular, with segmented 'boxcar' columns in the retinal vessels. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 20-month-old boy has leukocoria and a convergent squint. Which two presenting features are most common in retinoblastoma?","choices":["Leukocoria and strabismus","Tearing and lid swelling","Pain and photophobia","Redness and discharge","Proptosis and fever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a white pupil and a squint are the two classic presentations. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which surgical options are named for retinal detachment?","choices":["Trabeculoplasty or iridotomy","Intravitreal anti-VEGF alone","Temporal artery biopsy","Laser, cryotherapy, retinopexy or vitrectomy","Corneal transplantation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — chosen by type. Scleral buckle as well. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which triad defines Horner syndrome?","choices":["Ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis","Proptosis, chemosis and diplopia","Ptosis, exophthalmos and lid lag","Ptosis, mydriasis and diplopia","Miosis, photophobia and epiphora"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a sympathetic pathway lesion. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with a central retinal vein occlusion is being counselled. Which complication is he monitored for?","choices":["Neovascular glaucoma","Retinal detachment from a break","Corneal ulceration","Optic neuritis","Cataract within weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — ischaemia drives new vessel growth at the iris and angle. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 33"},{"q":"Why is lensectomy often deferred after a full-thickness eye wall laceration?","choices":["Because the cornea must heal first for access","Because the lens usually recovers on its own","Because the patient must be fasted for longer","Because implants are not available acutely","To let the hyphema and inflammation settle"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. And to measure accurately for an implant. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has amaurosis fugax from carotid emboli. Which treatments are named?","choices":["Topical pressure-lowering drops","Corticosteroids alone","Antiplatelets, with endarterectomy","Antibiotics","Intravitreal injection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — antiplatelet therapy for stroke risk, and surgery for the carotid source. Aspirin and clopidogrel. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with entropion has been using preservative-free artificial tears by day and lubricating ointment at night, with the lid taped. He asks whether this will fix the problem. What should he be told?","choices":["Only surgery repositions the lid","Taping alone is curative if continued for six months","It resolves spontaneously with age","The drops will gradually correct the lid position","Antibiotic drops will resolve it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The drops and taping protect the ocular surface; surgery is definitive. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which form of ophthalmoscopy is described as the most commonly used?","choices":["Fluorescein angiography","Direct ophthalmoscopy","Optical coherence tomography","Slit-lamp ophthalmoscopy","Indirect ophthalmoscopy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — alongside direct and indirect methods. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with open-angle glaucoma is not controlled on maximal topical therapy. What is the next step?","choices":["Immediate enucleation","Increase the same drops to eight times daily","Switch to a topical steroid","Observation with annual review","Laser trabeculoplasty"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the named escalation when medical therapy is refractory. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 21"}]};
